Z||000005||Black88GT||01-24-2001||09:34 AM||||Bump steer still happens on '88s.  Hit a big enough bump (there everywhere in the city) and you will feel a jerk.  With 17s its even worse.  I am hoping George will make a bump steer kit for 88s also so when I upgrade my wheels I can install it.<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Will||01-24-2001||04:38 PM||||Bump steer is purposely designed into the '88 suspension, while it is an accident in the early suspension.<P>'88 suspension <I>can</I> be corrected by moving the inner pivot of the lateral link out until it is aligned with the inner pivot of the toe link. This requires drilling a new hole in the mounting tabs, or something a litle more creative, but it is in principle simpler than fixing and early car. It also gives you ~1" greater track width in the rear.||||reg||1
